Omicsing Evolution: Toward the Deep
History of the Animal Kingdom
Our research explores how integrative omics-ranging from genomics
and
transcriptomics
to
metabolomics-can
illuminate
the
evolutionary
of animals, with
particular focus on the origins
and
diversification
Of
nervous
systems;
the
evolution
of
reproductive
modes
(both
sexual
and
asexual),
and
the
dynamic
interplay of symbiosis and co-evolution.
Drawing
on diverse model organisms such as Caenorhabditis elegans,
Drosophila; jellyfish; and choanoflagellates, we uncover how molecular
innovations and long-term evolutionary processes have shaped complex
traits and systems. By tracing ancestral gene networks and metabolic
pathways; our work bridges gaps between the single-celled precursors
Of multicellular life
and the intricate physiological and reproductive
strategies
observed
in
modern
animals.
Additionally;
long-term
evolution experiments provide windows into how these traits emerge,
adapt;
and
sometimes
converge
in
response
to
shifting
environmental
and
biological   pressures.
Together;
these
findings
underscore
the
power
of
omics-based   approaches
to
reconstruct
evolutionary
trajectories,
offering
transformative
insights
into
the mechanisms that underlie the remarkable complexity and diversity
of the animal kingdom
