강원대 세미나 발표 초록_구경아 (2).docx,2014년 08월 18일 08시 00분 00초,,20666,"세미나 주제: Coupling Mechanistic Simulation Modeling with GIS Landscape Modeling to Predict Climate Change Impacts on Red Spruce (Picea rubens Sarg.) in the Southern Appalachian Mountains, USA
발표자: 구경아 (경희대학교)
Red spruce is an ecologically and commercially important boreal and subboreal coniferous species in Eastern USA. Like other alpine and subalpine trees, it has low genetic diversity and is adapted to low optimum temperatures. It is considered vulnerable to climate warming. Good prediction of responses to climate change is considered critical for effective long-term management. Most predictions of climate change impacts on tree distribution are based on correlative niche models. Such predictions have been questioned because the models contain too little information about the complexities of ecological and physiological processes involved in actually producing range changes. These mechanisms include direct and indirect factors, and the latter especially are almost never taken fully into account. In this research we employed a three-phase complex-systems modeling approach to predict growth and distribution impacts of climate change on red spruce in the Great Smoky Mountains National Park (GSMNP). Each phase focused on Annual Radial Increment of growth and so our composite Model we call ARIM. In Phase 1, ARIM.NET, information and data assembly were achieved in the form of a qualitative network envirogram. This is in effect a conceptual model identifying all known direct and indirect factors influencing radial growth. In Phase 2, ARIM.SIM, the conceptual envirogram information was used to formulate a zero-dimensional simulation model employing the software Stella. This model quantified the envirogram information and simulations identified the dominant mechanisms determining habitat suitability and growth. In Phase 3, ARIM.HAB, the Stella simulation results were scaled up to two-dimensional, spatially distributed, landscape models of habitat suitability with ArcGIS software. Results showed low habitat suitability at higher elevations (1850-2028 m), medium suitability at lower elevations (1400-1650 m), and high suitability at intermediate elevations (1650-1850 m). Suitability was sensitive to elevation, slope, aspect, and distance to nearest stream at the lower levels, but only to elevation at higher levels, where air pollution factors such as increased acid precipitation and cloud immersion were dominant. We conclude that climate change in interaction with air pollution will cause red spruce habitats to shrink at both lower and higher elevations in GSMNP. Our complex-systems modeling approach is general, and extendable to other species and regions.",https://wwwk.kangwon.ac.kr/www/downloadBbsFile.do?atchmnflNo=15195&bbsNo=38&nttNo=20666&&pageUnit=10&searchCtgry=%EC%A0%84%EC%B2%B4%40%40%EC%B6%98%EC%B2%9C&key=279&pageIndex=134,[],강원대학교,백령광장(춘천),행사안내
