[환경연구소] 5월 7일(수) 정기세미나 개최 알림,2014년 04월 30일 08시 00분 00초,,20589,안녕하세요? 5월 7일(수) 오후 5시부터 아래와 같이 정기세미나를 개최합니다. 여러분들의 많은 관심과 참여 바랍니다. 가. 제 목 : Community patterns of benthic macroinvertebrates in the national scale in Korea 나. 연 사 : 박 영 석 교수(경희대학교 생물학과) 다. 일 시 : 2014년 5월 7일(수) 오후 5시 라. 장 소 : 환경연구소 세미나실 환경연구소장 이종범 드림(담당 신은진) ============================== 강원대학교 부속 환경연구소 강원도 춘천시 강원대학길 1 TEL : 033-250-7232 FAX : 033-259-5744 E-mail : envr@kangwon.ac.kr ==============================,https://wwwk.kangwon.ac.kr/www/selectBbsNttView.do?bbsNo=38&nttNo=20589&&pageUnit=10&searchCtgry=%EC%A0%84%EC%B2%B4%40%40%EC%B6%98%EC%B2%9C&key=279&pageIndex=141,['https://wwwk.kangwon.ac.kr/www/downloadBbsFile.do?atchmnflNo=15156&bbsNo=38&nttNo=20589&&pageUnit=10&searchCtgry=%EC%A0%84%EC%B2%B4%40%40%EC%B6%98%EC%B2%9C&key=279&pageIndex=141'],강원대학교,백령광장(춘천),행사안내
강원대세미나_20140507_초록.hwp,2014년 04월 30일 08시 00분 00초,,20589,"␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Community patterns of benthic macroinvertebrates in the national scale in Korea
Young-Seuk Park
Department of Biology, Kyung Hee University, Seoul
The community composition depends on stability of their habitats which provide the possibilities of development. The structures of community assemblages are potentially determined by various environmental factors acting on the habitats in different spatial and time scales. Regarding that different species show different spatial-temporal dynamics in responding to the impact of environments, understanding community abundance patterns is a fundamental step to initiate the sustainable management of aquatic ecosystems. Benthic macroinvertebrates were sampled with a Surber sampler from 1970 sampling sites on streams in Korea from 1997 to 2002. A self-organizing map (SOM) was used for patterning and visualizing characteristics of macroinvertebrate communities in the national scale, and accordingly classified the samples to 8 groups in a large scale. The groups corresponded to the geographical regions of sampling sites, distinguishing samples from mountain streams with high species richness and lowland areas with low species richness. Relations between species richness and abundance showed three patterns in the SOM groups, 1) high species richness and high abundance in clean area, 2) high abundance and intermediate species richness in intermediately organic polluted area, and 3) low species richness and low abundance in severely damaged area. Other biological indices were also clearly different between clusters. Finally, relations between species richness and abundance in large community data were effectively identified according to the similarities of community composition through the learning process of the SOM.

