‣ 예문
 ␀␀␀␀
Preventive Effect of Perilla Oil, Dietary n-3 Fatty Acid on Histological Brain Damage in Apo E(-/-) Mice Fed High Cholesterol Diet
Jeong-Eun Seong1*, Chan-Ho Oh2, Suk-Hoo Yoon3, Yeong-Ok Song1. 1Department of Food Science and Nutrition, Kimchi Research Institute, Pusan National University, Korea, 2Department of Food & Biotechnology, Woosuk University, 3Korea Food Research Institute
On recent studies, n-3 fatty acid alleviates the brain oxidative stress. This study is aimed for demonstrating the beneficial effects of perilla oil, rich in n-3 fatty acid on brain damage induced by high cholesterol diet. The diet contains 1.25% cholesterol and 16% fat with different oils. Lard, perilla, and sunflower oil were used as a dietary source for saturated, n-3, and n-6 fatty acid. Diet was provided for 10 weeks to the male C57BL/6 (Wild type, n=10) and ApoE KO mice (n=27). For histological analysis, brain tissue was sectioned followed by hematoxylin-eosin staining. Damaged cell was counted under the optical microscopy. In wild type, TC and TG accumulation in the brain of mice fed perilla oil diet (PE) were reduced by 3 and 43%, respectively than those for the group fed lard (Control). In ApoE KO PE, brain TC and TG concentration was reduced by 8 and 17%, respectively than the control (p<0.05). However, those concentrations of mice fed sunflower oil (SN) were not significantly different with the control. Brain damage was found to be less severe in the group of WPE, WL, APE, ASN, AL, in order. The degree of brain damage in PE of wild type and ApoE KO mice were significantly reduced by 35%, respectively, compared with the their respective control (p<0.05). However, degree of brain damage of SN was not significantly different with that for the control in ApoE KO mice. In conclusion, perilla oil rich in n-3 fatty acid might reduce brain damage in mice fed high cholesterol diet.
