강원대초록-손동환_1129_2010.hwp,2010년 11월 25일 08시 00분 00초,,19643,"␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Ginsenoside as a Prodrug
Dong Hwan Sohn
College of Pharmacy, Wonkwang University
Ginseng has been used as natural medication in the Far East over 2000 years. Ginseng is mainly treated to patient of weak condition in traditional Chinese medicine. The efficacy of Ginseng differs among individuals. 
It was reported that Rb1 was also undetectable in the serum obtained from rats administered with Rb1 orally. In spite of poor absorption of ginsenosides from the intestines, radioisotope assay revealed that the radioactivity of Rb2 in the serum after oral administration of Rb2 containing radioactive Rb2 to rats was 3 times higher than the level of intact Rb2 determined by HPLC, suggesting the presence of metabolites of Rb2 in the serum (Tanizawa et al., Proc. 6th Int. Ginseng Symp., Seoul, Sept. 1993, pp. 187-194). Recently, it is reported that intestinal bacterial metabolites M1, M8 and M11 are detected in the plasma after oral administratione of ginseng extract to humans, using a sensitive mass spectrometric method (Drug Metab Dispos, 8, 1065-71, 2003). 
Intestinal microflora is very changeable in dependence on host conditions such as diet, health and stress. The individual differences in ginseng efficacy may be partly associated with intestinal microflora of patients. Incubation of ginsenoside with human microflora resulted in the utilization of Rb1 during 3 h, Rg1 during 6 h, Rb2 and Rc during 12 h and Re during 24 h after incubation, deriving metabolic products M1-M11. After 12 h of incubation, characteristic metabolites M1-M4 were mainly observed. M2 and M3 were gradually metabolized to M1. Incubation of M1 for 72 h resulted in M12. 
Fecal specimens from 58 human healthy subjects were examined for Rb1-hydrolyzing potential. Seventy nine percent of the specimens showed positive reactions. However, the degree of M1 production markedly differed one another. To examine the relationship of bacterial Rb1-hydrolyzing potentials to antimetastatic activity of Rb1, it was administered to 2 groups of C57BL/6 mice with low and high hydrolyzing potentials, following the s.c. injection of LLC cells, and the inhibition rate of lung metastasis was compared between the two. In the mice with low hydrolyzing potential, the oral administration of Rb1 was ineffective in inhibiting lung metastasis; however, inhibitory activity of Rb1 expressed when administered to the mice with high hydrolyzing potential, suggesting the dependence the antimetastatic effects of Rb1 on bacterial Rb1-hydrolyzing potentials. This result allows us to speculate that it is very important to locate ginsenoside-hydrolyzing bacteria for the expression of ginseng pharmacological actions in the body.

