강원대초록.docx,2010년 11월 25일 08시 00분 00초,,19643,"Role of TRPV1 receptor in Psychological Dependence
Choon-Gon Jang
Department of Pharmacology, School of Pharmacy, Sungkyunkwan University, Suwon 440-746, Republic of Korea
The purpose of this study was to examine whether capsazepine, a TRPV1 antagonist, would inhibit the methamphetamine-induced rewarding effect using conditioned place preference (CPP) paradigm and self-administaration test in mice. Vehicle or capsazepine (2.5 mg/kg, 5 mg/kg, or 10 mg/kg, i.p.) was given at different phase of methamphetamine-induced CPP (development and reinstatement) and self administartion. Capsazepine 5 mg/kg significantly inhibited both development and reinstatement of methamphetamine-induced CPP and self-administartion. In study self-administered with methamphetamine, rats with extended access showed an increased latency to approach a chow pellet placed in the center of a novel environment, compared with short-access rats, at one-day withdrawal from emthamphetamine. At 14 days withdrawal, extended-access rats showed a decreased number of crossing and time spent in the center of an open field compared with drug-naïve rats. The data suggest that extended access to methamphetamine induced depressive-like and anxiety-like states in rats, which may underlie compulsive drug intake and motivation to seek for drug. Also our data suggest that TRPV1 plays an important role in methamphetamine rewarding effect.",https://wwwk.kangwon.ac.kr/www/downloadBbsFile.do?atchmnflNo=14617&bbsNo=38&nttNo=19643&&pageUnit=10&searchCtgry=%EC%A0%84%EC%B2%B4%40%40%EC%B6%98%EC%B2%9C&key=279&pageIndex=233,[],강원대학교,백령광장(춘천),행사안내
